-- title:
--   A common denominator clearing integral elements into d(j)bᵢ
-- statement:
--   Let $K_0$, $k$, $F$ be fields with $k$ a $K_0$-algebra and $F$ a $k$-algebra, and let $j \in F$ be transcendental over $k$, with $F$ finite-dimensional and separable over the intermediate field $k(j) = k(\{j\})$ generated by $j$. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $b : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to F$ be a family which is linearly independent over $k[j]$, in the sense that $\sum_i c_i(j)\,b_i = 0$ with $c_i \in k[X]$ forces all $c_i = 0$, and which spans $F$ up to polynomial denominators: every $x \in F$ satisfies $x\,q(j) = \sum_i c_i(j)\,b_i$ for some nonzero $q \in k[X]$ and some $c_i \in k[X]$. Let $d \in K_0[X]$ be nonzero and let $A$ assign to each $i$ an $n \times n$ matrix over $K_0[X]$ such that, for all $i, i'$, $b_i\,b_{i'}\,d(j) = \sum_{i''} (A_i)_{i'' i'}(j)\,b_{i''}$, the polynomials being evaluated at $j$ after transport of coefficients along $K_0 \to k$. Then there exists a nonzero $\Delta \in K_0[X]$ such that for every $x \in F$ integral over the $k$-subalgebra $k[j]$ generated by $j$ there are $c_i \in k[X]$ with $\Delta(j)\,x = \sum_i c_i(j)\,\bigl(d(j)\,b_i\bigr)$.
--
--   This is the classical discriminant argument in the form needed for function fields: the Gram determinant of the trace form of $F/k(j)$ on the scaled family $d(j)b_i$ provides a single denominator clearing all elements integral over $k[j]$ into the $k[j]$-lattice spanned by that family, and the point of the formulation is that the denominator can be taken with coefficients in the subfield $K_0$ once the multiplication table of the $b_i$ scaled by $d$ has coefficients in $K_0$. It is used in the construction of divisors on curves, where [`AlgebraicCurve.Divisor.exists_symmValue_rows_kernel_iff`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.Divisor.exists_symmValue_rows_kernel_iff) appeals to it.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Place_exists_gram_denominator_of_mulTable.lean

